<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Ocean Life and Conservation – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Ocean Life and Conservation</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Plants That Live in the Ocean</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a formal letter to request information. Interactive: Create a collaborative proposal for a class project.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose a formal letter requesting information about local marine conservation initiatives in Panama (e.g., "Request details about volunteering for an ocean cleanup event at Veracruz Beach or participating in a coral reef restoration project at Isla Iguanas.")
Can contribute to a group project description on protecting marine ecosystems (e.g., "We should mention how protecting marine life can help maintain a healthy environment.")
Can collaborate on a proposal for a project on ocean conservation (e.g., "We should reduce plastic use because it harms marine life and pollutes the oceans.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various images of "Plants That Live in the Ocean" such as "seaweed," "mangroves," and "plankton." Students are asked to quickly jot down 3-5 words or short phrases that come to mind for each image in their notebooks. Then, they share their words with a partner, discussing what they know about these plants and their importance in the "ocean ecosystem." The teacher encourages the use of vocabulary from previous lessons like "habitat," "species," and "marine life."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a few sentences on the board describing one of the plants, modeling the use of Present Simple for facts and Present Continuous for ongoing processes. For example: "Mangroves <i>protect</i> the "shoreline" and <i>are growing</i> in coastal areas." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "ecosystem" and "biodiversity," focusing on stress. The teacher also models how to write a simple factual sentence about a marine plant, e.g., "Seaweed is a type of plant that lives in the ocean."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and concepts related to marine plants and conservation importance.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do 'mangroves' grow in deep ocean waters or near the 'shoreline'?" (Expected answer: Near the shoreline.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are 'coral reefs' considered a 'habitat' for many 'species' of 'marine life'?" (Expected answer: Yes, they are.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents an example of a short, formal letter from a student requesting information about local marine conservation initiatives. This example letter will include phrases for requesting information and suggesting actions, using the target grammar (Present Simple, Present Continuous, Modals) and vocabulary. It will focus on initiatives related to marine plants, such as "coral reef restoration" or "mangrove preservation."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A projected example of a formal letter (digital or handout) with highlighted key phrases for formal requests, standard formal letter format (address, salutation, closing), and sections relevant to asking for information and proposing ideas. The letter will be addressed to a fictional local conservation organization in Panama.
                Genre: Formal Letter. Register: Formal. Format: Text document.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student will be able to interpret and understand the structure and purpose of a formal letter requesting information related to environmental conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the example letter individually.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main purpose of this letter?" (Expected answer: To request information about marine conservation projects.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match sections of the letter (e.g., salutation, body paragraph 1, body paragraph 2, closing) to their function (e.g., introducing the topic, asking for specific details, suggesting an idea, thanking the recipient).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing grammatically correct sentences and short paragraphs using the Present Simple (describing ocean life), Present Continuous (ongoing actions), and Modals for suggestions and obligations (e.g., "We <i>should protect</i> our oceans"). They will use target vocabulary such as "conservation," "protected zones," "species," "habitat," "mangroves," and "coral reefs" in the context of formal requests and project proposals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Cloze Activity):</b> The teacher provides a template of a formal letter with gaps. Students fill in the blanks using a word bank containing target vocabulary (e.g., "request," "information," "volunteer," "project," "conserve," "species," "ecosystem," "fragile") and appropriate verb forms (Present Simple/Continuous) or modals. Example sentences: "I would like to _______ information regarding..." / "Many 'marine' _______ are living in this 'fragile' 'ecosystem'..." / "We _______ 'protect' our 'coastal areas'..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled (Sentence Transformation):</b> Students are given informal requests or simple statements and transform them into formal requests or suggestions for a letter. For instance, "I want to know about your cleaning events" becomes "I would appreciate receiving details about your 'ocean cleanup' events." Or "We need to save the 'mangroves'" becomes "We 'should preserve' the 'mangroves' because they 'protect' the 'shoreline'." Students write these transformations in their notebooks. The teacher circulates, providing individual feedback on formality and grammar.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Peer Sentence Construction & Check):</b> In pairs, students formulate 2-3 sentences for a hypothetical project proposal related to "Plants That Live in the Ocean." They must include at least one modal verb ("should," "must") and two vocabulary words from the list (e.g., "We 'should' create a 'Public Service Announcement' about 'plankton's' importance." or "We 'must' work to 'rehabilitate' 'endangered' 'coral reefs'."). After writing, pairs exchange sentences and peer-check for correct grammar, vocabulary use, and clarity. Teacher monitors and facilitates.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task using the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will individually compose a formal letter to a local Panamanian marine conservation organization (e.g., Fundación MarViva or ANCON) requesting specific information about their initiatives concerning "Plants That Live in the Ocean." The letter must include:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A clear introduction stating the purpose of the letter.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Specific questions about "volunteering" for an 'ocean cleanup event' at Veracruz Beach or participating in a 'coral reef restoration project' at Isla Iguanas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A paragraph suggesting a collaborative class project on ocean conservation, specifically mentioning the protection of "marine ecosystems" and "plants" like "mangroves" or "seaweed." They should use modals for suggestions (e.g., "We 'should reduce plastic use' because it 'harms marine life' and 'pollutes' the 'oceans'." or "We 'should establish more marine protected areas' to help 'preserve ocean biodiversity'.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A formal closing.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual written product (formal letter). Students will draft their letters on paper or a digital document. The teacher provides a checklist for essential components and target language use.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> The letters directly address "Ocean Life and Conservation" by focusing on local initiatives and "Plants That Live in the Ocean."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion:</b> While the primary focus is written fluency and accuracy, students can briefly discuss their project ideas with a partner before writing to ensure their suggestions are clear and well-articulated, incorporating pragmatic functions like "Proposing conservation actions" and "Describing ecological importance."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment and self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Rubric. The teacher will use a checklist to assess each student's letter for the inclusion of required components (formal address, clear purpose, specific questions, project proposal). A simple rubric will evaluate grammar accuracy (Present Simple, Present Continuous, Modals), appropriate vocabulary use (e.g., "conservation," "ecosystem," "mangroves"), and overall clarity and formality of the letter.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their formal letters requesting information and proposing a class project on ocean conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Formality:</b> The letter clearly communicates its purpose and adheres to formal writing conventions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Correct application of Present Simple, Present Continuous, and Modals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate and accurate use of at least 5-7 theme-related vocabulary words (e.g., "marine," "habitat," "conservation," "mangroves," "species," "pollute," "preserve").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> All required parts of the letter (introduction, specific questions, project proposal) are present and developed.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the formal letter was most challenging for you (e.g., choosing formal language, structuring sentences, remembering vocabulary)?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two new words or phrases you used in your letter that you didn't know how to write formally before this lesson."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>(Review and plan for next steps)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about writing a formal letter to request information or propose an idea?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific thing you want to improve in your English writing for our next lesson?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of the lesson worked particularly well to support students' writing skills (e.g., the letter template, specific grammar practice)? What areas need more scaffolding or adjustment for future writing tasks, especially concerning formal register and vocabulary specific to "Ocean Life and Conservation"? Were the instructions for the formal letter clear enough for A2.4 learners? How can I better integrate pronunciation practice (e.g., stress in "environmental") even in a writing-focused lesson, perhaps by having students read their proposals aloud to a partner?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The formal letters written in this lesson, especially the proposed class projects, will serve as a foundational input for Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration. Students will use the information gathered and their project proposals to develop and present a collaborative "21st Century Skills Project" focusing on a "Public Service Announcement (PSA)" or a presentation to "conserve" and "sustain" marine plant life, incorporating information from the formal letters and previous lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>